I am getting ready to design and implement a computer adventure game, but 
before I start I want to find out what type of game people like.  If you could 
please take the time to answer the following questions and email the results 
to me at jrtodd@phoenix.princeton.edu, I would be very appreciative.

Thanks,
Jason Todd


Background Questions:

1.   Which do you enjoy more, reading a good book or watching television?  
2.   Why?

3.   How many hours a week do you spend reading unassigned books?

4.   If not limited by time constraints, how many hours a week would you
     spend reading?

5.   How many hours a week do you spend watching television?

6.   If not limited by time constraints, how many hours a week would you
     spend watching television?

7.   Do you think you have a good imagination?

8.   Have you ever played Doom or 3-D Castle Wolfenstein or games like
     them (pseudo 3-D animated games where you see through the main
     character's eyes)?  
9.   Did you enjoy it?

10.  Have you ever played a text based adventure game like Zork or a MUD? 
11.  Did you enjoy it?

12.  When using a computer, do you prefer a text-based interface (like DOS
     or a UNIX command shell) or a point-and-click interface (like the
     Macintosh Operating System)?

13.  How many straight hours can you sit and watch television if not limited
     by time constraints?  
14.  What factors do you think limit this time?

15.  How many straight hours can you sit and read a good book if not limited
     by time constraints?  What factors do you think limit this time?

16.  How many straight hours can you play your favorite computer game if
     not limited by time constraints?  
17.  What factors do you think limit this time?

18.  What gender are you (male or female)?

19.  How old are you?  __under 18   __18-25  __26-35  __36-45  __46-55  
       __56-65  __over 65


Interface Questions:

Assuming technology is not a barrier, please consider the following interface 
models.  For each interface type, please rank it from 1 (least playable) to 9 
(most playable) for playability (how easy or natural you think it would be to 
play a game with that interface).  Also, rank each interface from 1 to 9 for 
how much you think you would enjoy playing a game with that interface.  
Finally, please write down how many hours you think you could spend playing a 
game like that.

Output:
	Text-based output:  The room is described in words to you.  All action
		in the room is conveyed via descriptive language.  For example:

		As you reach the top of the incline you find you have come to a
		small clearing in the forest that is ringed by trees.  At the 
		eastern side of the ring there appears to be a small cave like
		dwelling around which are the scattered remains of various 
		animals and humanoid creatures.  The tracks lead into the 
		opening of the cave.
		The foul, rancid odor of rotting meat sears your nostrils and
		burns your eyes, causing them to water.
		A low husky growling comes from deep within the cave.

	20.  How playable do you think this interface is on a scale of 1 (least
	     playable) to 9 (most playable)?
	21.  On a scale of 1 (least enjoyable) to 9 (most enjoyable), how much
	     do you think you would like a game with this type of interface?
	22.  How many hours in a row do you think you would play a game
	     with this type of interface if there were no time constraints?

	Impoverished graphics output:  The room is shown in a crude 3-D
		representation using colored polygons.  There are no shadows or
		textures to objects.  People in the game are represented in 
		sort of stick figure type shapes.  Actions that occur in the 
		room are shown crudely on the screen.

	23.  How playable do you think this interface is on a scale of 1 (least
	     playable) to 9 (most playable)?
	24.  On a scale of 1 (least enjoyable) to 9 (most enjoyable), how much
	     do you think you would like a game with this type of interface?
	25.  How many hours in a row do you think you would play a game
	     with this type of interface if there were no time constraints?

	Realistic Graphics Output:  The room looks real.  There are shadows
		and textures to objects.  People have skin and hair and 
		clothes.  Characters on the screen are seen to do the things 
		they are doing.

	26.  How playable do you think this interface is on a scale of 1 (least
	     playable) to 9 (most playable)?
	27.  On a scale of 1 (least enjoyable) to 9 (most enjoyable), how much 
	     do you think you would like a game with this type of interface?
	28.  How many hours in a row do you think you would play a game
	     with this type of interface if there were no time constraints?

Perspective:
	If the game is graphic based, there are two options for viewing
	perspectives: from the eyes of your character or from some external
	source.

	Internal:  You see the world as if you were looking through your
		character's eyes.  You can't see your face unless you look in a
		mirror.

	29.  How playable do you think this interface is on a scale of 1 (least
	     playable) to 9 (most playable)?
	30.  On a scale of 1 (least enjoyable) to 9 (most enjoyable), how much
